,"ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.acids.h.001","deckId":"chemistry.acids","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A dilute solution in a Philippine lab has [H⁺] = 10⁻³ mol/L. Using pH = −log₁₀[H⁺], what is its pH?","answer":"3","explanation":"The negative base-ten logarithm of 10⁻³ is 3, so the pH is 3.","scienceFact":"The negative base-ten logarithm of 10⁻³ is 3, so the pH is 3.","topic":"chemistry.acids","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Acids, bases, indicators and the pH scale. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"chemistry.acids.h.002","deckId":"chemistry.acids","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Cebu lab compares pH 2 and pH 4 dilute solutions. How many times greater is [H⁺] at pH 2?","answer":"100","explanation":"Two pH units mean a factor of 10². The pH 2 sample has 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration.","scienceFact":"Two pH units mean a factor of 10². The pH 2 sample has 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration.","topic":"chemistry.acids","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Acids, bases, indicators and the pH scale. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"times","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"chemistry.acids.h.003","deckId":"chemistry.acids","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Manila lab dilutes a pH 3 strong acid tenfold. Assume full dissociation and negligible water contribution. What is its approximate new pH?","answer":"4","explanation":"Tenfold dilution lowers the hydrogen-ion concentration by a factor of ten. The pH rises by one unit, from 3 to 4.","scienceFact":"Tenfold dilution lowers the hydrogen-ion concentration by a factor of ten. The pH rises by one unit, from 3 to 4.","topic":"chemistry.acids","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Acids, bases, indicators and the pH scale. Content aligns wit