ation","misconception":"chemistry.bonding.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Mass number","misconception":"chemistry.bonding.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"An empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of elements in a compound. It need not show a full molecule.","scienceFact":"An empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of elements in a compound. It need not show a full molecule.","topic":"chemistry.bonding","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Chemical bonds, compounds and biomolecules. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"molecule"},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.001","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What causes the pressure of a gas in a container?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Collisions with the walls"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Particles resting at the bottom","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Gravity switching off","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"No particle motion","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Gas particles move and collide with container walls. The force of these impacts per area produces pressure.","scienceFact":"Gas particles move and collide with container walls. The force of these impacts per area produces pressure.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.002","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"At fixed temperature, how does gas volume respond when pressure doubles?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Volume becomes zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Volume halves"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Volume doubles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Volume stays fixed","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.2.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Boyle’s law links pressure and volume inversely when temperature and amount of gas are fixed.","scienceFact":"Boyle’s law links pressure and volume inversely when temperature and amount of gas are fixed.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.003","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which temperature scale is required in gas-law ratios?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Fahrenheit only","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Any scale gives the same ratio","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Kelvin"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Celsius only","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.3.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Gas-law temperature ratios use kelvin, an absolute temperature scale. Celsius ratios do not give the same physical result.","scienceFact":"Gas-law temperature ratios use kelvin, an absolute temperature scale. Celsius ratios do not give the same physical result.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.004","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"At fixed pressure, what happens to gas volume when kelvin temperature doubles?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Volume halves","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Volume stays the same","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Volume drops to zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Volume doubles"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Charles’s law makes gas volume proportional to kelvin temperature when pressure and gas amount are fixed.","scienceFact":"Charles’s law makes gas volume proportional to kelvin temperature when pressure and gas amount are fixed.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.005","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What model treats gas particles as widely spaced and moving randomly?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Kinetic particle model"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Fixed crystal lattice","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Stationary packed particles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only a continuous solid","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","scienceFact":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.006","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"As gas warms, what happens to its particles’ average kinetic energy?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"It must become zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"It increases"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"It decreases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"It stays unchanged","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"For an ideal gas, average kinetic energy rises in step with kelvin temperature.","scienceFact":"For an ideal gas, average kinetic energy rises in step with kelvin temperature.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.007","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to pressure when a fixed-volume gas warms?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Pressure vanishes","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure is unrelated to temperature","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pressure increases"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Pressure decreases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"At fixed volume, warmer gas particles cause greater pressure. Do not heat sealed aerosol cans.","scienceFact":"At fixed volume, warmer gas particles cause greater pressure. Do not heat sealed aerosol cans.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.008","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens when a gas gains more space at fixed temperature?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Pressure increases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure doubles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pressure becomes negative","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Pressure decreases"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"With more volume at fixed temperature and gas amount, particles hit the walls less often per area, so pressure falls.","scienceFact":"With more volume at fixed temperature and gas amount, particles hit the walls less often per area, so pressure falls.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.009","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of pressure?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Pascal"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Joule","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Watt","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Kelvin","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","scienceFact":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.e.010","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What must remain fixed when applying Boyle’s law to a gas sample?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only the timer reading","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Temperature and amount of gas"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Both pressure and volume","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only the co