ure and gas amount are fixed.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.005","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A student draws a gas as tiny moving particles with large spaces between them. What model is being used?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only a continuous solid","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Kinetic particle model"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Fixed crystal lattice","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Stationary packed particles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","scienceFact":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.006","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A sealed gas warms up. What happens to the average kinetic energy of its particles?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"It stays unchanged","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"It must become zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"It increases"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"It decreases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"For an ideal gas, average kinetic energy rises in step with kelvin temperature.","scienceFact":"For an ideal gas,