:"With more volume at fixed temperature and gas amount, particles hit the walls less often per area, so pressure falls.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.009","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A force of one newton acts on one square meter. What pressure unit does this define?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Kelvin","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pascal"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Joule","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Watt","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","scienceFact":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.010","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A student changes pressure and measures volume for Boyle’s law. Which conditions need control?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only the container color","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only the timer reading","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Temperature and amount of gas"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Both pressure and volume","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Boyle’s law assumes constant temperature and gas amount. Changing either can change the pressure-volume relationship.","scienceFact":"Boyle’s law assumes constant temperature and gas amount. Changing either can change the pressure-volume relationship.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.011","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"order","prompt":"Order a careful Boyle’s law trial.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"Seal a fixed gas amount"},{"id":"step-1","text":"Keep temperature steady"},{"id":"step-2","text":"Change pressure"},{"id":"step-3","text":"Measure the new volume"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2","step-3"],"explanation":"Seal a fixed gas amount. Keep temperature steady. Change pressure. Measure the new volume. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Diffusion is the net spreading of particles from higher to lower concentration. Random particle motion drives it.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.m.012","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"medium","type":"match","prompt":"Pair each gas-law variable with its SI unit.","answer":{"Pressure":"Pascal","Volume":"Cubic meter","Temperature":"Kelvin"},"explanation":"Each pair links a science idea to its correct role. Check the matched pairs to see how the ideas fit.","scienceFact":"At constant volume and temperature, adding gas particles increases pressure because more particles strike the walls.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.001","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Cebu lab seals a gas at 100 kPa and 0.04 m³. At fixed temperature, pressure rises to 200 kPa. What volume results?","answer":"0.02","explanation":"Boyle’s law gives V₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ P₂ = 100 × 0.04 ÷ 200 = 0.02 m³.","scienceFact":"Boyle’s law gives V₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ P₂ = 100 × 0.04 ÷ 200 = 0.02 m³.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"m³","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.002","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Philippine gas sample has volume 0.03 m³ at 300 K. At fixed pressure, it warms to 400 K. What is its volume?","answer":"0.04","explanation":"Charles’s law gives V₂ = V₁T₂ ÷ T₁ = 0.03 × 400 ÷ 300 = 0.04 m³.","scienceFact":"Charles’s law gives V₂ = V₁T₂ ÷ T₁ = 0.03 × 400 ÷ 300 = 0.04 m³.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"m³","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.003","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Manila lab holds a gas at fixed volume. Pressure is 100 kPa at 300 K. What is its pressure at 600 K?","answer":"200","explanation":"At fixed volume, P/T is constant for a fixed ideal gas amount. Doubling kelvin temperature doubles pressure.","scienceFact":"At fixed volume, P/T is constant for a fixed ideal gas amount. Doubling kelvin temperature doubles pressure.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"kPa","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.004","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 2 m³ gas sample warms from 200 K to 400 K at fixed pressure. Which change fits Charles’s law?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Volume drops to zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Volume doubles"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Volume halves","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Volume stays the same","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.4.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Charles’s law makes gas volume proportional to kelvin temperature when pressure and gas amount are fixed.","scienceFact":"Charles’s law makes gas volume proportional to kelvin temperature when pressure and gas amount are fixed.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.005","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A model uses random particle motion and impacts to explain gas pressure. What is this model called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Stationary packed particles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only a continuous solid","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Kinetic particle model"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Fixed crystal lattice","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.5.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","scienceFact":"The particle model describes gas using moving particles. Their spacing and impacts help explain pressure and volume.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.006","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two samples of one ideal gas have different kelvin temperatures. The hotter sample has which change in average particle energy?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"It decreases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"It stays unchanged","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"It must become zero","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"It increases"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"For an ideal gas, average kinetic energy rises in step with kelvin temperature.","scienceFact":"For an ideal gas, average kinetic energy rises in step with kelvin temperature.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.007","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"At constant volume and gas amount, the kelvin temperature doubles. What change in pressure does the ideal gas model predict?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Pressure increases"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure decreases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pressure vanishes","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Pressure is unrelated to temperature","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.7.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"At fixed volume, warmer gas particles cause greater pressure. Do not heat sealed aerosol cans.","scienceFact":"At fixed volume, warmer gas particles cause greater pressure. Do not heat sealed aerosol cans.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.008","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A sealed gas expands from 2 m³ to 4 m³ at fixed temperature. Which pressure trend fits the particle model?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Pressure becomes negative","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure decreases"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pressure increases","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Pressure doubles","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.8.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"With more volume at fixed temperature and gas amount, particles hit the walls less often per area, so pressure falls.","scienceFact":"With more volume at fixed temperature and gas amount, particles hit the walls less often per area, so pressure falls.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.009","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A data sheet gives pressure as newtons per square meter. Which named SI unit is equivalent?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Watt","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Kelvin","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pascal"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Joule","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.9.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","scienceFact":"One pascal equals one newton per square meter. It is the SI unit of pressure.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.010","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Gas escapes while temperature changes during compression. Which pair must instead stay fixed for a valid Boyle comparison?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Both pressure and volume","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only the container color","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only the timer reading","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Temperature and amount of gas"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Boyle’s law assumes constant temperature and gas amount. Changing either can change the pressure-volume relationship.","scienceFact":"Boyle’s law assumes constant temperature and gas amount. Changing either can change the pressure-volume relationship.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.011","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"order","prompt":"Put these temperatures in order from coldest to hottest.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"100 K"},{"id":"step-1","text":"200 K"},{"id":"step-2","text":"300 K"},{"id":"step-3","text":"400 K"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2","step-3"],"explanation":"100 K. 200 K. 300 K. 400 K. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Diffusion is the net spreading of particles from higher to lower concentration. Random particle motion drives it.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.gases.h.012","deckId":"chemistry.gases","level":"hard","type":"diagram","prompt":"Two equal containers hold the same gas at the same temperature. One has twice as many particles. Which quantity is greater there?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Particle size","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.12.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Container volume","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Temperature by definition","misconception":"chemistry.gases.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"At constant volume and temperature, adding gas particles increases pressure because more particles strike the walls.","scienceFact":"At constant volume and temperature, adding gas particles increases pressure because more particles strike the walls.","topic":"chemistry.gases","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Pressure, volume, temperature and ideal gas models. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"gas"},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.001","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What state of matter keeps both its shape and volume?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Solid"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Liquid","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Gas","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Plasma","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"A solid has a fixed shape and volume under usual conditions. Its particles stay close and vibrate in place.","scienceFact":"A solid has a fixed shape and volume under usual conditions. Its particles stay close and vibrate in place.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.002","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the substance that dissolves another substance called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Residue","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Solvent"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Solute","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Suspension","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.2.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"The solvent dissolves a solute to form a solution. In salt water, water is the solvent.","scienceFact":"The solvent dissolves a solute to form a solution. In salt water, water is the solvent.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.003","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What method separates sand from water with a paper barrier?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Melting","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Freezing","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Filtration"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Dissolving","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.3.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"A filter traps solid pieces that do not dissolve. Liquid can pass through its small holes.","scienceFact":"A filter traps solid pieces that do not dissolve. Liquid can pass through its small holes.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.004","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is a mixture that looks uniform throughout called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Coarse suspension","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pure element","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Single compound","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Solution"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"A solution is a uniform mixture. Its solute particles are dispersed through the solvent.","scienceFact":"A solution is a uniform mixture. Its solute particles are dispersed through the solvent.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.005","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which change forms a new substance?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Chemical change"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Change of state only","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Simple cutting","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Physical mixing only","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"A chemical change forms one or more new substances. Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water.","scienceFact":"A chemical change forms one or more new substances. Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.006","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the change from liquid to gas at a surface called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Deposition","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Evaporation"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Condensation","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Freezing","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Evaporation occurs when particles escape from a liquid’s surface into the gas phase.","scienceFact":"Evaporation occurs when particles escape from a liquid’s surface into the gas phase.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.007","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the change from gas to liquid called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Sublimation","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Condensation"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Melting","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.7.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Water vapor can condense into liquid on a cool surface. The drops do not pass through the bottle wall.","scienceFact":"Water vapor can condense into liquid on a cool surface. The drops do not pass through the bottle wall.","topic":"chemistry.matter","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Properties, states, solutions and safe separation. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"chemistry.matter.e.008","deckId":"chemistry.matter","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which mixture can often be separated with a magnet?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Salt and water","misconception":"chemistry.matter.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Sugar and water",