pine and Pacific volcanoes near plate boundaries. Which named zone fits this pattern?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Sahara Desert belt","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Arctic ice circle","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.1.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Pacific Ring of Fire"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Mid-Atlantic Ridge only","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.1.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Many plate edges surround the Pacific Ocean. This Ring of Fire has many volcanoes and earthquakes.","scienceFact":"Many plate edges surround the Pacific Ocean. This Ring of Fire has many volcanoes and earthquakes.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"data":{"headers":["Map observation","Location"],"rows":[["Many volcanoes","Pacific plate edges"],["Many earthquakes","Pacific plate edges"]]}},{"id":"earth.plates.h.002","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"data","prompt":"A Philippine offshore profile shows a trench and a plate bending downward below another. Which boundary type fits?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Divergent","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"No plate boundary","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.2.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only a weather front","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Convergent"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"At a convergent boundary, plates move toward one another. An oceanic plate may subduct beneath another plate.","scienceFact":"At a convergent boundary, plates move toward one another. An oceanic plate may subduct beneath another plate.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"data":{"headers":["Offshore profile","Record"],"rows":[["Surface feature","Deep trench"],["Plate motion","Toward another plate and down"]]}},{"id":"earth.plates.h.003","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"data","prompt":"A Philippine fault map shows sideways offsets without a broad spreading ridge. Which boundary-motion type is closest?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Transform"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Divergent","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.3.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only collision without sliding","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only vertical convection","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.3.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"At transform boundaries, plates slide past one another. Many strike-slip faults show similar sideways motion.","scienceFact":"At transform boundaries, plates slide past one another. Many strike-slip faults show similar sideways motion.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"data":{"headers":["Fault model","Record"],"rows":[["Motion","Mainly sideways"],["Rock layer markers","Offset across a fault"]]}},{"id":"earth.plates.h.004","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Earth’s plates include both crust and rigid uppermost mantle. Which layer contains the full plates?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Inner core","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Lithosphere"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Liquid outer core","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Atmosphere","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.4.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"The lithosphere includes the crust and the rigid uppermost mantle. It is divided into tectonic plates.","scienceFact":"The lithosphere includes the crust and the rigid uppermost mantle. It is divided into tectonic plates.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.005","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Seismic evidence supports mostly solid rock, not a global liquid ocean, below the crust. Which layer is being described?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Atmosphere","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Ocean","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.5.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Mantle"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Outer core","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.5.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"The mantle is mostly solid rock. Over long times, hot mantle material can deform and flow.","scienceFact":"The mantle is mostly solid rock. Over long times, hot mantle material can deform and flow.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.006","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"S-waves do not pass through a deep layer, while other evidence supports moving metal there. Which major layer fits?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Crust","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Rigid lithosphere","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Inner core","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Outer core"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"The outer core is liquid metal, mainly iron and nickel. The inner core is solid under very high pressure.","scienceFact":"The outer core is liquid metal, mainly iron and nickel. The inner core is solid under very high pressure.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.007","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A map shows a long ocean ridge with young crust at its center and older crust farther away. Which boundary type fits?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Divergent"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Transform","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only a collision zone","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Stationary interior only","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.7.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"At a divergent boundary, plates move apart. Magma can rise and form new crust at spreading ridges.","scienceFact":"At a divergent boundary, plates move apart. Magma can rise and form new crust at spreading ridges.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.008","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A cross-section shows a plate slanting into the mantle beneath a volcanic arc. Which process explains the geometry?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Solar fusion","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Subduction"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Deposition by wind","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.8.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Subduction carries one plate beneath another into the mantle. It can produce trenches, earthquakes and volcanic arcs.","scienceFact":"Subduction carries one plate beneath another into the mantle. It can produce trenches, earthquakes and volcanic arcs.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.009","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A fossil species appears in matching-age rocks on separated continents that fit together. Which explanation best links both observations?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Unrelated random maps only","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"No past movement","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Former continental connection"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Fossils swimming across every ocean","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.9.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Matching fossils and rock layers suggest a past link between lands now apart. Their shapes can also give clues.","scienceFact":"Matching fossils and rock layers suggest a past link between lands now apart. Their shapes can also give clues.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.010","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two locked blocks store elastic energy while plates keep moving. Which change releases a seismic disturbance?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"All mantle rock becomes liquid","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Wind pushes the ground","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"The Moon touches Earth","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Sudden fault slip releases energy"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Faults can lock while stress builds. Sudden slip releases stored energy as seismic waves.","scienceFact":"Faults can lock while stress builds. Sudden slip releases stored energy as seismic waves.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.011","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"order","prompt":"Order a simple oceanic subduction sequence.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"Plates converge"},{"id":"step-1","text":"An oceanic plate bends down"},{"id":"step-2","text":"The plate descends into the mantle"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2"],"explanation":"Plates converge. An oceanic plate bends down. The plate descends into the mantle. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"The crust is Earth’s thin outer rocky layer. Oceanic and continental crust differ in thickness and composition.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.plates.h.012","deckId":"earth.plates","level":"hard","type":"diagram","prompt":"A mantle model shows heat transfer by slow bulk movement over long times. Which process fits?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only photosynthesis","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.12.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Convection"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Reflection","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only conduction with no movement","misconception":"earth.plates.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Mantle convection involves slow material motion driven by density differences. Slab pull and ridge forces also contribute to plate motion.","scienceFact":"Mantle convection involves slow material motion driven by density differences. Slab pull and ridge forces also contribute to plate motion.","topic":"earth.plates","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Earth’s layers and tectonic plate boundaries. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"plates"},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.001","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What rock group forms when lava from a Philippine volcano cools?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Igneous"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Sedimentary","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Metamorphic only","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Unchanged soil","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and solidifies. Cooling conditions affect their texture.","scienceFact":"Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and solidifies. Cooling conditions affect their texture.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.002","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process breaks rock into smaller pieces at the rice terraces?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Nuclear fusion","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Weathering"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only erosion","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Condensation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.2.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Weathering breaks down rock in place. Erosion transports the loosened material elsewhere.","scienceFact":"Weathering breaks down rock in place. Erosion transports the loosened material elsewhere.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.003","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process carries loosened soil away from a Philippine hillside?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only crystallization","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only condensation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Erosion"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only weathering in place","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.3.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Erosion moves rock or soil by agents such as water, wind, ice or gravity. Plant cover can help reduce it.","scienceFact":"Erosion moves rock or soil by agents such as water, wind, ice or gravity. Plant cover can help reduce it.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.004","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which rock group forms when sediments become compacted and cemented?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Igneous","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Metamorphic only","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Molten magma","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Sedimentary"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Sedimentary rocks can form from sediments that are compacted and cemented. Other types form from chemical or biological deposits.","scienceFact":"Sedimentary rocks can form from sediments that are compacted and cemented. Other types form from chemical or biological deposits.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.005","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which rock group forms when heat and pressure change existing rock without full melting?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Metamorphic"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only igneous","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Loose sediment","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Unchanged sand","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks change under heat, pressure or fluids without fully melting.","scienceFact":"Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks change under heat, pressure or fluids without fully melting.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.006","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process changes liquid surface water to vapor?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only infiltration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Evaporation"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Precipitation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Freezing","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Evaporation moves water from a liquid surface into the air as vapor. Energy helps water molecules escape.","scienceFact":"Evaporation moves water from a liquid surface into the air as vapor. Energy helps water molecules escape.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.007","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process forms liquid droplets from water vapor?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Melting","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Sublimation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Condensation"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Condensation changes water vapor into liquid droplets. It is important in cloud and dew formation.","scienceFact":"Condensation changes water vapor into liquid droplets. It is important in cloud and dew formation.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.008","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is water falling from clouds called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Transpiration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Infiltration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Precipitation"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Precipitation is water falling from clouds, including rain, snow and hail.","scienceFact":"Precipitation is water falling from clouds, including rain, snow and hail.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.009","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is water entering the ground from the surface called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Infiltration"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Condensation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only cloud formation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Infiltration is water moving from the surface into the ground. Soil type and plant cover can affect its rate.","scienceFact":"Infiltration is water moving from the surface into the ground. Soil type and plant cover can affect its rate.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.010","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What provides most energy that drives evaporation in the water cycle?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only sound from waves","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"The Sun"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only the Moon’s reflected light","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only Earth’s iron core","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Solar energy drives much of the evaporation in the water cycle. Gravity helps return and move water toward lower levels.","scienceFact":"Solar energy drives much of the evaporation in the water cycle. Gravity helps return and move water toward lower levels.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"water"},{"id":"earth.rocks.e.011","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"easy","type":"input","prompt":"What is water flowing over land toward streams called?","answer":"Runoff","e