Area":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.006","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Water leaves a lake and enters the air without the lake boiling. What process is this?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Freezing","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only infiltration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Evaporation"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Precipitation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.6.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Evaporation moves water from a liquid surface into the air as vapor. Energy helps water molecules escape.","scienceFact":"Evaporation moves water from a liquid surface into the air as vapor. Energy helps water molecules escape.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.007","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Moist air cools and tiny droplets form on a cool leaf. Which water-cycle process occurs?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Melting","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Sublimation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Condensation"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Condensation changes water vapor into liquid droplets. It is important in cloud and dew formation.","scienceFact":"Condensation changes water vapor into liquid droplets. It is important in cloud and dew formation.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.008","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Rain falls from a cloud onto the ground. What water-cycle term describes it?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Precipitation"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Transpiration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Infiltration","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.8.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Precipitation is water falling from clouds, including rain, snow and hail.","scienceFact":"Precipitation is water falling from clouds, including rain, snow and hail.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.009","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Rain soaks into soil instead of flowing away over it. What process occurs?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only cloud formation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Infiltration"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Evaporation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Condensation","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.9.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Infiltration is water moving from the surface into the ground. Soil type and plant cover can affect its rate.","scienceFact":"Infiltration is water moving from the surface into the ground. Soil type and plant cover can affect its rate.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.010","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A pond warms and loses water during a sunny day. Which energy source drives much of this change?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only Earth’s iron core","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only sound from waves","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"The Sun"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only the Moon’s reflected light","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.10.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Solar energy drives much of the evaporation in the water cycle. Gravity helps return and move water toward lower levels.","scienceFact":"Solar energy drives much of the evaporation in the water cycle. Gravity helps return and move water toward lower levels.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.011","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"order","prompt":"Trace one possible route of water from sea to rain and back.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"Ocean water evaporates"},{"id":"step-1","text":"Cloud droplets form"},{"id":"step-2","text":"Rain falls"},{"id":"step-3","text":"Runoff returns to the sea"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2","step-3"],"explanation":"Ocean water evaporates. Cloud droplets form. Rain falls. Runoff returns to the sea. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Runoff is water flowing over land. It can carry sediment and join streams, rivers and other bodies of water.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.m.012","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"medium","type":"match","prompt":"Pair each rock group with its formation process.","answer":{"Igneous":"Melt cools and solidifies","Sedimentary":"Sediment is compacted and cemented","Metamorphic":"Existing rock changes without fully melting"},"explanation":"Each pair links a science idea to its correct role. Check the matched pairs to see how the ideas fit.","scienceFact":"A mineral is a natural inorganic solid with a characteristic composition and ordered atomic structure. Rocks are often mixtures of minerals.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.rocks.h.001","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"hard","type":"data","prompt":"A Philippine rock has interlocking crystals formed from cooled melt. Which rock group best fits?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Metamorphic only","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Unchanged soil","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Igneous"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Sedimentary","misconception":"earth.rocks.mixup.1.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and solidifies. Cooling conditions affect their texture.","scienceFact":"Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and solidifies. Cooling conditions affect their texture.","topic":"earth.rocks","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Rock formation, weathering and the water cycle. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"data":{"headers":["Philippine rock sample","Record"],"rows":[["Starting material","Molten lava"],["Formation","Cools and becomes solid"]]}},{"id":"earth.rocks.h.002","deckId":"earth.rocks","level":"hard","type":"data","prompt":"Rock beside an Ifugao terrace breaks in place without the pieces being carried away. Which process has occurred?","options