ast quarter. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Jupiter is the largest planet in the Solar System by diameter and mass. It is a gas giant.","topic":"earth.solar","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Planetary motion, moon phases, eclipses and seasons. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"earth.solar.h.012","deckId":"earth.solar","level":"hard","type":"diagram","prompt":"A newly observed rocky object is gravitationally bound to a planet and formed naturally. What is it called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Galaxy","misconception":"earth.solar.mixup.12.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Natural satellite"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Artificial satellite","misconception":"earth.solar.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Main-sequence star","misconception":"earth.solar.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"A natural satellite, or moon, orbits a planet or another nonstellar body. Our Moon orbits Earth.","scienceFact":"A natural satellite, or moon, orbits a planet or another nonstellar body. Our Moon orbits Earth.","topic":"earth.solar","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Planetary motion, moon phases, eclipses and seasons. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"solar"},{"id":"earth.stars.e.001","deckId":"earth.stars","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What physical process powers the Sun?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Nuclear fusion"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Burning wood","misconception":"earth.stars.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Nuclear fission of coal","misconception":"earth.stars.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only chemical rusting","misconception":"earth.stars.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"The Sun’s core fuses hydrogen nuclei into helium. Fusion releases energy that eventually leaves as light and other radiati