s?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Biotic factor","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Consumer population","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Living tissue","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Abiotic factor"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Abiotic factors are nonliving features such as light, water and temperature. They affect living things.","scienceFact":"Abiotic factors are nonliving features such as light, water and temperature. They affect living things.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.007","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A population grows, then levels off as resources become scarce. Which idea explains the upper limit?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Carrying capacity"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Unlimited growth","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Mutation rate","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Trophic level","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.7.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"A habitat has a limit to the life it can support over time. Food, water and space help set that limit.","scienceFact":"A habitat has a limit to the life it can support over time. Food, water and space help set that limit.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.008","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Both species breed more successfully when together than apart. Which interaction best fits this evidence?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Competition","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Mutualism"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Parasitism","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Predation","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.8.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"In mutualism, both species benefit from the link. Bees can get nectar as flowers gain a way to move pollen.","scienceFact":"In mutualism, both species benefit from the link. Bees can get nectar as flowers gain a way to move pollen.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.009","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Plants with the same soil and water grow poorly when crowded beneath a canopy. What resource is most limited?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Gravity","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Body heat","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Light"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"DNA","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.9.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Plants can compete for light, water, space and nutrients. A dense canopy can reduce the light below it.","scienceFact":"Plants can compete for light, water, space and nutrients. A dense canopy can reduce the light below it.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.010","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Each feeding level uses energy for life tasks and releases heat. What explains the smaller energy supply above it?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Energy is created by predators","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"All food becomes new tissue","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Nutrients vanish permanently","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Energy is used and dispersed as heat"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Organisms use energy for life processes. Much leaves as heat, so only part moves to the next feeding level.","scienceFact":"Organisms use energy for life processes. Much leaves as heat, so only part moves to the next feeding level.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.011","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"order","prompt":"Order these events in leaf-litter nutrient recycling.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"Leaves fall"},{"id":"step-1","text":"Decomposers break them down"},{"id":"step-2","text":"Nutrients enter soil"},{"id":"step-3","text":"Roots absorb nutrients"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2","step-3"],"explanation":"Leaves fall. Decomposers break them down. Nutrients enter soil. Roots absorb nutrients. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"A population is a group of the same species living in the same area at the same time.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.ecosystems.h.012","deckId":"life.ecosystems","level":"hard","type":"diagram","prompt":"A species occurs naturally on one island and nowhere else. Which term fits this evidence?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Abiotic","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.12.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Endemic"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Domesticated","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Invasive everywhere","misconception":"life.ecosystems.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"An endemic species is native to a limited region. Protecting its habitat is important for its survival.","scienceFact":"An endemic species is native to a limited region. Protecting its habitat is important for its survival.","topic":"life.ecosystems","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Energy flow, biodiversity and Philippine ecosystems. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"foodweb"},{"id":"life.genetics.e.001","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What molecule stores inherited instructions in rice plants?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"DNA"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Starch","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Water","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Cellulose","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"DNA stores genetic information. Its sequence helps guide the making of proteins and inherited traits.","scienceFact":"DNA stores genetic information. Its sequence helps guide the making of proteins and inherited traits.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.002","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is a version of a gene called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Enzyme","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Allele"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Tissue","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Organelle","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"An allele is a version of a gene. A diploid organism usually has two alleles at a gene location.","scienceFact":"An allele is a version of a gene. A diploid organism usually has two alleles at a gene location.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.003","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is an organism’s expressed trait called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Chromosome","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Gamete","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Phenotype"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Genotype","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Phenotype means the traits we can observe or measure. Genes and the environment can both affect it.","scienceFact":"Phenotype means the traits we can observe or measure. Genes and the environment can both affect it.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.004","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which term describes having two different alleles for a gene?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Homozygous","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Recessive only","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Identical alleles","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Heterozygous"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"A heterozygous genotype has two different alleles at the same gene location.","scienceFact":"A heterozygous genotype has two different alleles at the same gene location.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.005","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which cell division halves the chromosome number in gametes?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Meiosis"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Mitosis","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Osmosis","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Budding","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Meiosis halves the chromosome number. Fertilization joins two gametes and restores the diploid number.","scienceFact":"Meiosis halves the chromosome number. Fertilization joins two gametes and restores the diploid number.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.006","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is a lasting change in a DNA sequence called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Condensation","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Mutation"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Digestion","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Diffusion","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"A mutation changes a DNA sequence. Its effect can be harmful, helpful or neutral in a given setting.","scienceFact":"A mutation changes a DNA sequence. Its effect can be harmful, helpful or neutral in a given setting.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.007","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which group changes over generations during evolution?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"One organ","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"One body cell","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Population"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Single individual","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.7.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Populations evolve as inherited traits change in frequency across generations. Individuals do not evolve during their lives.","scienceFact":"Populations evolve as inherited traits change in frequency across generations. Individuals do not evolve during their lives.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.008","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which process can spread helpful inherited traits across generations?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Need-based change","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Deliberate mutation","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Instant adaptation","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Natural selection"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Some inherited traits help organisms survive and breed in a given place. These traits can become more common over time.","scienceFact":"Some inherited traits help organisms survive and breed in a given place. These traits can become more common over time.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.009","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"In an Aa × Aa cross, what is the chance of aa?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"1/4"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"1/2","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"3/4","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"1","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.9.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"One of the four equally likely allele pairs is aa. This predicts a probability, not an exact family count.","scienceFact":"One of the four equally likely allele pairs is aa. This predicts a probability, not an exact family count.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.e.010","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What can shared limb bone plans suggest?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"No genetic link","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Shared ancestry"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Identical habitats","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Equal body sizes","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.10.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"A shared bone plan can point to a common ancestor. The parts may now do different jobs.","scienceFact":"A shared bone plan can point to a common ancestor. The parts may now do different jobs.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"punnett"},{"id":"life.genetics.e.011","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"input","prompt":"What are the bundles of DNA and proteins in a nucleus called?","answer":"Chromosomes","explanation":"Chromosomes are DNA and protein structures. They carry many genes and are copied before cell division.","scienceFact":"Chromosomes are DNA and protein structures. They carry many genes and are copied before cell division.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"accept":["chromosomes","Chromosomes"]},{"id":"life.genetics.e.012","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"easy","type":"input","prompt":"What term means differences among members of one species?","answer":"Variation","explanation":"Variation means differences among organisms. Inherited variation gives natural selection material to act on.","scienceFact":"Variation means differences among organisms. Inherited variation gives natural selection material to act on.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"accept":["variation","Variation"]},{"id":"life.genetics.m.001","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A rice seed grows with traits from its parents. Which molecule carries these instructions?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Cellulose","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"DNA"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Starch","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Water","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.1.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"DNA stores genetic information. Its sequence helps guide the making of proteins and inherited traits.","scienceFact":"DNA stores genetic information. Its sequence helps guide the making of proteins and inherited traits.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.m.002","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two rice plants have different forms of a gene for a trait. What are these forms called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Organelle","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Enzyme","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Allele"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Tissue","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.2.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"An allele is a version of a gene. A diploid organism usually has two alleles at a gene location.","scienceFact":"An allele is a version of a gene. A diploid organism usually has two alleles at a gene location.","topic":"life.genetics","gradeBand":10,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Heredity, variation and natural selection. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"life.genetics.m.003","deckId":"life.genetics","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A Philippine garden pea plant has purple flowers. What term names this observed trait?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Genotype","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Chromosome","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Gamete","misconception":"life.genetics.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Phenotype"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Phenotype means the traits we can observe or measure. Genes and the environment can both affect it.","scienceFact":"Phenotype means the traits we can ob