Velocity stays constant"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Motion must stop at once","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Speed must rise","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.5.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"With zero net force, velocity does not change. An object may be at rest or move at constant velocity.","scienceFact":"With zero net force, velocity does not change. An object may be at rest or move at constant velocity.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.006","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A sliding book slows on a rough table. Which force is mainly responsible?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only gravity","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only air buoyancy","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Friction"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Nuclear force","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.6.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Sliding friction acts against motion between surfaces in contact. It can warm the surfaces as the object slows.","scienceFact":"Sliding friction acts against motion between surfaces in contact. It can warm the surfaces as the object slows.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.007","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"An astronaut keeps the same mass on the Moon but feels a smaller downward pull. Which quantity has changed?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Mass","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Volume by definition","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Atom count","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Weight"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Weight is gravitational force and is measured in newtons. Mass is measured in kilograms and does not depend on local gravity.","scienceFact":"Weight is gravitational force and is measured in newtons. Mass is measured in kilograms and does not depend on local gravity.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.008","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A heavy and light ball drop together with no air resistance. How do their accelerations compare?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Equal gravitational acceleration"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"The heavier ball has greater acceleration","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"The lighter ball has no gravity","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only the heavy ball falls","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.8.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"At the same place near Earth, freely falling objects have equal acceleration when air resistance is ignored.","scienceFact":"At the same place near Earth, freely falling objects have equal acceleration when air resistance is ignored.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.009","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A book rests on a table without sinking through it. Which contact force supports it?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Nuclear force","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Normal force"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Weight parallel to the ramp","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only air drag","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.9.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"A normal force is a contact force perpendicular to a surface. Its size depends on the other forces present.","scienceFact":"A normal force is a contact force perpendicular to a surface. Its size depends on the other forces present.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.010","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A balance gives a reading of 5 kg for an object. Which quantity is measured?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Acceleration","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Pressure","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Mass"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Weight","misconception":"physics.forces.mixup.10.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Mass is measured in kilograms. In a given interaction, greater mass means greater resistance to acceleration.","scienceFact":"Mass is measured in kilograms. In a given interaction, greater mass means greater resistance to acceleration.","topic":"physics.forces","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Newton’s laws, friction, gravity and net force. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.forces.m.011","deckId":"physics.forces","level":"medium","type":"order","prompt":"Order these net forces from least to greatest magnitude.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"0 N"},{"id":"step-1","text":"2 N"},{"i