nd":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.m.011","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"medium","type":"order","prompt":"Order these speeds from slowest to fastest.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"1 m/s"},{"id":"step-1","text":"3 m/s"},{"id":"step-2","text":"6 m/s"},{"id":"step-3","text":"9 m/s"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2","step-3"],"explanation":"1 m/s. 3 m/s. 6 m/s. 9 m/s. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Speed uses distance per time. The SI unit is meters per second, written m/s.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.m.012","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"medium","type":"match","prompt":"Link each motion quantity to its meaning.","answer":{"Speed":"Distance per time","Velocity":"Speed with direction","Acceleration":"Velocity change per time"},"explanation":"Each pair links a science idea to its correct role. Check the matched pairs to see how the ideas fit.","scienceFact":"Motion is described relative to a reference frame. Different frames can give different descriptions of the same motion.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"apply","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.001","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A jeepney travels 180 m in 15 s, then stops for 5 s. What is its average speed for the whole 20 s?","answer":"9","explanation":"Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 180 ÷ 20 = 9 m/s. Include the stop time.","scienceFact":"Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 180 ÷ 20 = 9 m/s. Include the stop time.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"physics.motion.h.002","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A cyclist in Cebu goes 60 m east, then 20 m west in 10 s. What is the eastward average velocity?","answer":"4","explanation":"Net eastward displacement is 60 − 20 = 40 m. Average velocity is 40 ÷ 10 = 4 m/s east.","scienceFact":"Net eastward displacement is 60 − 20 = 40 m. Average velocity is 40 ÷ 10 = 4 m/s east.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"physics.motion.h.003","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"calc","prompt":"A Manila cart changes velocity from 2 m/s east to 10 m/s east in 4 s. What is its eastward acceleration?","answer":"2","explanation":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = (10 − 2) ÷ 4 = 2 m/s² east.","scienceFact":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = (10 − 2) ÷ 4 = 2 m/s² east.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true,"unit":"m/s²","tolerance":0.001},{"id":"physics.motion.h.004","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A walker goes 5 m east, then 5 m west. The path is 10 m, but which directed change is zero?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Average speed","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Displacement"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Distance traveled","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Elapsed time","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.4.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Displacement links start and end positions with a direction. Returning to the start gives zero displacement.","scienceFact":"Displacement links start and end positions with a direction. Returning to the start gives zero displacement.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.005","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two straight lines show different distances gained in equal times. Which quantity is greater for the steeper line?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Temperature","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Energy unit","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.5.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Speed"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Mass","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.5.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"The slope of a distance-time graph gives speed. A steeper rising line means more distance per time.","scienceFact":"The slope of a distance-time graph gives speed. A steeper rising line means more distance per time.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.006","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A graph lists 4 m at 2 s, 3 s and 4 s. Which motion description fits this interval?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"The object speeds up","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"The object moves faster each second","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"The object has infinite speed","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"The object is at rest"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"A flat distance-time line means distance is not changing during the interval, so the object is at rest.","scienceFact":"A flat distance-time line means distance is not changing during the interval, so the object is at rest.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.007","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A projectile travels upward and sideways with no air resistance. Which force still acts throughout its flight?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Gravity"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"A lasting hand push","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"No force at the top","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"A forward fuel force","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.7.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Ignoring air resistance, gravity is the only force on a projectile after release. It acts downward throughout the flight.","scienceFact":"Ignoring air resistance, gravity is the only force on a projectile after release. It acts downward throughout the flight.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.008","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A projectile has no horizontal force acting on it. Which horizontal motion prediction follows?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"It reverses at the top","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.8.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"It stays constant"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"It becomes zero at the top","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"It grows because of gravity","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.8.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"With no horizontal net force, the horizontal component of velocity remains constant. Gravity changes the vertical component.","scienceFact":"With no horizontal net force, the horizontal component of velocity remains constant. Gravity changes the vertical component.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.009","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A point moves in a circle at constant speed. Its direction changes each moment. Is it accelerating?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"No, motion must stop","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.9.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Yes, mass must increase","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Yes, its velocity changes direction"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"No, speed must rise","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.9.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Acceleration is a change in velocity. Turning changes velocity even when speed stays the same.","scienceFact":"Acceleration is a change in velocity. Turning changes velocity even when speed stays the same.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.010","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"mcq","prompt":"A rectangular area under a velocity-time plot is 12 m. Which motion quantity does that signed area represent?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Acceleration","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.10.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Mass","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.10.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Force","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.10.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Displacement"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"The signed area under a velocity-time graph gives displacement over the time interval.","scienceFact":"The signed area under a velocity-time graph gives displacement over the time interval.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.011","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"order","prompt":"Order the height of a ball during its flight, from launch to landing.","options":[{"id":"step-0","text":"Ball rises"},{"id":"step-1","text":"Ball reaches its highest point"},{"id":"step-2","text":"Ball falls"}],"answer":["step-0","step-1","step-2"],"explanation":"Ball rises. Ball reaches its highest point. Ball falls. Each stage leads to the next.","scienceFact":"Speed uses distance per time. The SI unit is meters per second, written m/s.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.motion.h.012","deckId":"physics.motion","level":"hard","type":"diagram","prompt":"Two observers give different descriptions of the same passenger’s motion. Which choice explains the difference?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only eye color","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.12.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Reference frame"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only body mass","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.12.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only air temperature","misconception":"physics.motion.mixup.12.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Motion is described relative to a reference frame. Different frames can give different descriptions of the same motion.","scienceFact":"Motion is described relative to a reference frame. Different frames can give different descriptions of the same motion.","topic":"physics.motion","gradeBand":9,"bloom":"analyze","curriculumArea":"Speed, velocity, acceleration and projectiles. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true,"diagram":"distance"},{"id":"physics.waves.e.001","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does sound need in order to travel?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"A material medium"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Empty space alone","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.1.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Light alone","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.1.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"No matter or energy","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.1.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Sound is a mechanical wave and needs matter to travel. It cannot travel through a vacuum.","scienceFact":"Sound is a mechanical wave and needs matter to travel. It cannot travel through a vacuum.","topic":"physics.waves","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Wave speed, frequency, sound and vibration. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.waves.e.002","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which wave property mainly sets the pitch of a kulintang note?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Only source size","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.2.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Frequency"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Only amplitude","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.2.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only loudness","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.2.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Pitch is closely related to frequency. Greater frequency usually gives a higher perceived pitch.","scienceFact":"Pitch is closely related to frequency. Greater frequency usually gives a higher perceived pitch.","topic":"physics.waves","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Wave speed, frequency, sound and vibration. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":true,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.waves.e.003","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which wave property is distance from rest to a crest?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Wavelength","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Period only","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Amplitude"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Frequency only","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.3.1"}],"answer":"choice-2","explanation":"Amplitude is the maximum displacement from the rest position. It is not the distance from crest to trough.","scienceFact":"Amplitude is the maximum displacement from the rest position. It is not the distance from crest to trough.","topic":"physics.waves","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Wave speed, frequency, sound and vibration. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.waves.e.004","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the distance between adjacent wave crests called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Amplitude","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Frequency","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Loudness","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.4.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Wavelength"}],"answer":"choice-3","explanation":"Wavelength is the distance between matching points on successive cycles, such as neighboring crests.","scienceFact":"Wavelength is the distance between matching points on successive cycles, such as neighboring crests.","topic":"physics.waves","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Wave speed, frequency, sound and vibration. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.waves.e.005","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the number of complete wave cycles per second called?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Frequency"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Wavelength","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Amplitude","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Wave speed only","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"Frequency counts complete cycles per second. Its SI unit is hertz, written Hz.","scienceFact":"Frequency counts complete cycles per second. Its SI unit is hertz, written Hz.","topic":"physics.waves","gradeBand":8,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"Wave speed, frequency, sound and vibration. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"physics.waves.e.006","deckId":"physics.waves","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula links wave speed, frequency and wavelength?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"v = f + λ","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"v = f × λ"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"v = f / λ","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"v = λ / f","misconception":"physics.waves.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength. The units combine as per second times meters to give meters per second.","scienceFact":"Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength. The units combine as per second times meters to give meters per second.","topic":