e kilogram?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"1000"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"100","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"10","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.5.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"0.001","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.5.3"}],"answer":"choice-0","explanation":"One kilogram equals one thousand grams. The prefix kilo- means one thousand.","scienceFact":"One kilogram equals one thousand grams. The prefix kilo- means one thousand.","topic":"skills.measurement","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"SI units, measurement, uncertainty and conversions. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"skills.measurement.e.006","deckId":"skills.measurement","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Watt","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Kelvin"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Degree Celsius only","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.6.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Joule","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.6.2"}],"answer":"choice-1","explanation":"Kelvin is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature. Gas-law ratios use absolute temperature in kelvin.","scienceFact":"Kelvin is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature. Gas-law ratios use absolute temperature in kelvin.","topic":"skills.measurement","gradeBand":7,"bloom":"remember","curriculumArea":"SI units, measurement, uncertainty and conversions. Content aligns with these curriculum areas; no official competency codes are claimed.","ph":false,"reviewed":true},{"id":"skills.measurement.e.007","deckId":"skills.measurement","level":"easy","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where should the eye be when reading a liquid scale?","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Far below the cylinder","misconception":"skills.measurement.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-1","tex